# Building differentiated apps for Android XR with 3D content

## Android XR
you can have headsets or glasses, they have two modes

* Home Space: You have several apps oppened


* Full Space: You can only have one app open and it will provide a "bubble" view. Here you use the "SceneCore API" in the Jetpack XR SDK to take advantage of the spatial capabilities of Android XR. Here we have `SkyBox` and `Geometry`, you can think of Skybox as a distant image like a photosphere that surrounds you, it creates the illusion of a distant background. `Geometry` on the other hand, adds shape and depth, this creates a parallax effect, usually used to show the ground and objects near the user

    In full space you have the option to display videos in 180 or 360 degrees 

[This](goo.gle/xr-home-to-full) a guide on transitioning form Home space to Full space and [this](goo.gle/haxr) is a Hello Android XR sample

## Jetpack SceneCore Library
Provides the APIs that lets you create objects in 3D space. Elementes created in this context are called "Entity", The entity for showing immersive videos is called `SurfaceEntity`

## Pose
A pose represents the position/translation and orientation/rotation of an entity in 3D space

## 3D Models
Android XR uses glTF to display complex 3D objects

### Blender
Tool to create and animate glTF files

## ARCore mobile devices
Helps your app understand the real world around the user, make use of perception capabilities, you have features like "persisten anchors" which lets you anchor an object in a place in the full space

Has APIs to let the user interact with the app using their hands

